					<description><![CDATA[<div><img width="300" height="200" src="https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/iStock-954683756-min-2-300x200.jpg" class="attachment-medium size-medium wp-post-image" alt="" style="margin-bottom: 15px;" srcset="https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/iStock-954683756-min-2-300x200.jpg 300w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/iStock-954683756-min-2-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/iStock-954683756-min-2-768x512.jpg 768w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/iStock-954683756-min-2-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/iStock-954683756-min-2-194x129.jpg 194w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/iStock-954683756-min-2.jpg 1920w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /></div>
<p>Overview As part of our continued goal of helping developers provide safer products for businesses and consumers, we here at McAfee Advanced Threat Research (ATR) recently investigated temi, a teleconference robot produced by Robotemi Global Ltd. Our research led us to discover four separate vulnerabilities in the temi robot, which this paper will describe in [&#8230;]</p>
