<p>The unpacked malware is a binary file of 32 bits that can be found as an EXE file.</p>
<p><img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-103803" src="/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/information-sample.png" alt="" width="778" height="388" srcset="https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/information-sample.png 778w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/information-sample-300x150.png 300w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/information-sample-768x383.png 768w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/information-sample-205x102.png 205w" sizes="(max-width: 778px) 100vw, 778px" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Figure 7. Information sample of the malware</em></p>
<p>The malware’s first action is to combine all the required functions it needs into one large function, combining the modules already loaded in Windows with additional DLLs as described below.</p>
<p>Instead of searching for the function in the usual way, the malware makes a CRC32 hash of the name of each function and compares with hardcoded values. Additionally, instead of using the function “GetProcAddress”, the malware uses the Process Environment Block (PEB) to make analysis harder.</p>
<p><img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-103806" src="/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/using-CRC32.png" alt="" width="902" height="726" srcset="https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/using-CRC32.png 902w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/using-CRC32-300x241.png 300w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/using-CRC32-768x618.png 768w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/using-CRC32-160x129.png 160w" sizes="(max-width: 902px) 100vw, 902px" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Figure 8. Get the module accessing the PEB and using a CRC32</em></p>
<p>If the module cannot be discovered, it will load with “LdrLoadDll”, a native function of Windows, to try avoiding hooks in the usual functions, e.g. “LoadLibraryW”:</p>
<p><img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-103809" src="/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/load-library.png" alt="" width="902" height="290" srcset="https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/load-library.png 902w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/load-library-300x96.png 300w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/load-library-768x247.png 768w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/load-library-205x66.png 205w" sizes="(max-width: 902px) 100vw, 902px" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Figure 9. Load library using LdrLoadDll</em></p>
<p><img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-103812" src="/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-functions-from-module.png" alt="" width="902" height="588" srcset="https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-functions-from-module.png 902w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-functions-from-module-300x196.png 300w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-functions-from-module-768x501.png 768w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-functions-from-module-198x129.png 198w" sizes="(max-width: 902px) 100vw, 902px" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Figure 10. Get functions from module, e.g. using a CRC32 hash</em></p>
<p>If the malware fails to get a function, it will go to a “sleep” call and terminate itself.</p>
<p>Later, the malware extracts the configuration file from a resource with a custom type and a custom name using the functions “FindResourceA”, “LockResource”, “LoadResource” and “SizeOfResource”. The file extracted in memory is decrypted using the RC4 algorithm with a hardcoded key in the resource.</p>
<p>The struct of the resource is:</p>
<ul>
<li>4 bytes -&gt; The size of the hardcoded key to decrypt the configuration file.</li>
<li>Variable size -&gt; the hardcoded key to decrypt the configuration file.</li>
<li>Variable size -&gt; the configuration file encrypted.</li>
</ul>
<p>The malware reads the first 4 bytes and reserves memory with the size of the password and reserves memory of the resource minus 4 bytes and the size of the password. Finally, it decrypts the configuration file:</p>
<p><img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-103815" src="/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-configuration-file.png" alt="" width="902" height="570" srcset="https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-configuration-file.png 902w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-configuration-file-300x190.png 300w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-configuration-file-768x485.png 768w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-configuration-file-205x129.png 205w" sizes="(max-width: 902px) 100vw, 902px" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Figure 11. Get configuration file and decrypt it</em></p>
<p>If the malware fails to get the configuration file, it will terminate itself.</p>
<p>After getting the configuration file, the malware will parse it and save the fields in memory and write in the registry information to encrypt the files in the machine. The malware will try first to write in the registry-hive &#8220;HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE” but if it cannot create it, it will use the registry-hive “HKEY_CURRENT_USER”:</p>
<p><img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-103818" src="/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/write-in-the-registry.png" alt="" width="902" height="452" srcset="https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/write-in-the-registry.png 902w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/write-in-the-registry-300x150.png 300w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/write-in-the-registry-768x385.png 768w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/write-in-the-registry-205x103.png 205w" sizes="(max-width: 902px) 100vw, 902px" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Figure 12. Write in the registry</em></p>
<p>After the writing in the registry has been completed, it will get some privileges using a token as SE_DEBUG_PRIVILEGE and SE_IMPERSONATE_PRIVILEGE:</p>
<p><img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-103821" src="/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-privileges.png" alt="" width="902" height="542" srcset="https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-privileges.png 902w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-privileges-300x180.png 300w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-privileges-768x461.png 768w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-privileges-205x123.png 205w" sizes="(max-width: 902px) 100vw, 902px" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Figure 13. Get some special privileges in the token</em></p>
<p>Later, the malware creates three threads, one to get information about the machine, such as the operating system version, one to get processes and the last one to get services in the system.</p>
<p>After this step, it will get the system directory and use “VSSadmin” to delete the Volume Shadow copies of the system. Volume Shadow copies can contain copies of the encrypted files and would be an option to restore from if no backup exists.</p>
<p><img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-103824" src="/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/delete-shadow-volumes.png" alt="" width="902" height="394" srcset="https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/delete-shadow-volumes.png 902w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/delete-shadow-volumes-300x131.png 300w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/delete-shadow-volumes-768x335.png 768w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/delete-shadow-volumes-205x90.png 205w" sizes="(max-width: 902px) 100vw, 902px" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Figure 14. Delete the shadow volumes</em></p>
<p>Later, the malware will enumerate the logical units, prepare the new extension for the future encrypted files, based on the size that is defined in the ransomware config with a random extension, and encrypt all files in the fixed type units and remote units with the new extension.</p>
<p><img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-103827" src="/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/crypt-the-file.png" alt="" width="902" height="424" srcset="https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/crypt-the-file.png 902w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/crypt-the-file-300x141.png 300w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/crypt-the-file-768x361.png 768w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/crypt-the-file-205x96.png 205w" sizes="(max-width: 902px) 100vw, 902px" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Figure 15. Crypt the files</em></p>
<p>After all these steps have been completed, it will create the ransom note on the desktop using the functions “SHGetFolderPathlW” and “CreateFileW”. Subsequently, it will write the ransom note from the memory into a new file with the function “WriteFile”. The malware will create the ransom note in the root folder (for example “c:\”) of each logical unit. Next, it will launch “notepad.exe” with an argument to the ransom note file to show the user what happened on the system:</p>
<p><img class="aligncenter wp-image-103830 size-full" src="/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/creation-of-ransom-note.png" alt="" width="902" height="638" srcset="https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/creation-of-ransom-note.png 902w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/creation-of-ransom-note-300x212.png 300w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/creation-of-ransom-note-768x543.png 768w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/creation-of-ransom-note-182x129.png 182w" sizes="(max-width: 902px) 100vw, 902px" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Figure 16. Creation of the ransom note in the desktop and root units</em></p>
<p>Finally, after the encryption of the files and creation of the ransom note, the malware creates a bat file in the %temp% folder of the machine with a temporary name and writes the content to destroy itself using the program “taskkill”. The batch script will delete the malware sample with its path using the command “del” and finally delete the bat file with the command “del %0%”. Of course, as the malware uses the “del” command without destroying itself before the deletion, it can be recovered with some forensic tools with luck (the same can also be said for the bat file).</p>
<p>This way the malware tries to remove itself from the machine to avoid being detected and analyzed by security researchers:</p>
<p><img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-103833" src="/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-temp-path.png" alt="" width="902" height="506" srcset="https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-temp-path.png 902w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-temp-path-300x168.png 300w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-temp-path-768x431.png 768w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/get-temp-path-205x115.png 205w" sizes="(max-width: 902px) 100vw, 902px" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Figure 17. Get Temp path and make a temporary file as a bat and launch it</em></p>
<p>Finally, the malware will finish with “ExitProcess”.</p>
