<p>According to the Microsoft documentation, w15 defines the PersistentDocumentId Class. When the object is serialized out as xml, its qualified name is w15:docId. The 128-bit GUID is set as an ST_Guid attribute which, according to the Microsoft documentation, refers to a unique token. The used class generates a GUID for use as the DocID and generates the associated key. The client stores the GUID in that structure and persists in the doc file. If, for example, we would create a document and would “Save As”, the w15:docId GUID would persist across to the newly created document. What would that mean for our list above? Documents with the same GUID value need to be placed in chronological order and then we can state the earliest document is the root for the rest, for example:</p>
<p><img class="aligncenter wp-image-103233 size-full" src="/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/table.png" alt="" width="804" height="492" srcset="https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/table.png 804w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/table-300x184.png 300w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/table-768x470.png 768w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/table-205x125.png 205w" sizes="(max-width: 804px) 100vw, 804px" /></p>
<p>What we can say from above table is that ‘_IFG_536R.docx” was the first document we observed and that later documents with the same docID value were created from the same base document.</p>
<p>To add to this assertion; in the settings.xml file the value “rsid” (Revision Identifier for Style Definition) can be found. According to Microsoft’s documentation: <em>“This element specifies a unique four-digit number which shall be used to determine the editing session in which this style definition was last modified. This value shall follow this following constraint: All document elements which specify the same rsid* values shall correspond to changes made during the same editing session. An editing session is defined as the period of editing which takes place between any two subsequent save actions.”</em></p>
<p>Let’s start with the rsid element values from “*_IFG_536R.docx”:</p>
<p><img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-103236" src="/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/rsids.png" alt="" width="532" height="334" srcset="https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/rsids.png 532w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/rsids-300x188.png 300w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/rsids-205x129.png 205w" sizes="(max-width: 532px) 100vw, 532px" /></p>
<p>And compare with the rsid element values from “*_PMS.docx”:</p>
<p><img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-103239" src="/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/rsid.png" alt="" width="532" height="350" srcset="https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/rsid.png 532w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/rsid-300x197.png 300w, https://www.mcafee.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/rsid-196x129.png 196w" sizes="(max-width: 532px) 100vw, 532px" /></p>
<p>The rsid elements are identical for the first four editing sessions for both documents. This indicates that these documents, although they are now separate, originated from the same document.</p>
<p>Digging into more values and metadata (we are aware they can be manipulated), we created the following overview in chronological order based on the creation date:</p>
